  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Given that the equivalence $P⇔Q$ is true, we have that $P$ is a *necessary and sufficient condition* for $Q.$  \n\nIn our case, we need to find the conditions that are *necessary and sufficient* for the given one. In other words,\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-8=0)\\,⇒\\,\\text{Condition}\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-8=0)\\,⇐\\,\\text{Condition}.\n$$\n\nNote that the solutions to the given equation are\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+2x-8 & =0 \\\\ (x+4)(x-2) & =0 \\\\ x & =-4,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's inspect each of the given conditions.\n\n- Condition I is *not* a necessary condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-8=0)\\,⇏\\,(x=-4)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the number $x=2,$ the statement ${x}^{2}+2x-8=0$ is true but $x\\ne -4.$\n\n- Condition II is *not* a sufficient condition:\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-8=0)\\,⇍\\,(|x|\\le 2)\\,\\,\\times \n$$\n\nFor example, for the number $x=0,$ the statement $|x|\\le 2$ is true but $x=0$ is not a solution to ${x}^{2}+2x-8=0.$\n\n- Condition III is both necessary and sufficient: The statement ${x}^{2}+2x-8=0$ is true *if and only if* $x=-2$ or $x=4.$\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-8=0)\\,⇒\\,(x=-2\\text{ or }x=4)\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-8=0)\\,⇐\\,(x=-2\\text{ or }x=4).\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+2x-8=0)\\,⇔\\,(x=-2\\text{ or }x=4)\\,\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"",
